1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,807
A bus speedometer.

2
00:00:01,807 --> 00:00:03,328
A bowler's speed gun.

3
00:00:03,328 --> 00:00:05,062
The meter on a bicycle.

4
00:00:05,462 --> 00:00:07,334
All three tell you how fast.

5
00:00:07,334 --> 00:00:09,401
None of them tells you how far.

6
00:00:09,851 --> 00:00:13,641
Aarav rides to college with a
speed meter on his handlebar.

7
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:15,599
It never shows distance.

8
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:18,358
So can he still find out how far he rode?

9
00:00:18,858 --> 00:00:19,758
Yes.

10
00:00:19,355 --> 00:00:22,968
Draw his readings as a graph,
and the graph tells him.

11
00:00:22,968 --> 00:00:24,019
Let's see how.

12
00:00:24,669 --> 00:00:27,006
So what is a velocity-time graph?

13
00:00:27,356 --> 00:00:29,203
Time goes along the bottom.

14
00:00:29,203 --> 00:00:31,002
Velocity goes up the side.

15
00:00:31,402 --> 00:00:33,100
Each point is one reading.

16
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,890
At this moment, this fast.

17
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:37,567
A position-time graph tells us where.

18
00:00:37,567 --> 00:00:40,486
This graph tells us how
fast, and which way.

19
00:00:41,136 --> 00:00:44,163
Let's watch Aarav's ride
in the motion notebook.

20
00:00:44,513 --> 00:00:49,400
He starts from rest and speeds up, to
six metres per second in four seconds.

21
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:50,591
The line climbs.

22
00:00:50,991 --> 00:00:54,144
Then he cruises at a steady
six metres per second.

23
00:00:54,144 --> 00:00:55,500
The line goes flat.

24
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:59,189
Near the gate he brakes,
and stops in three seconds.

25
00:00:59,189 --> 00:01:01,051
The line comes down to zero.

26
00:01:01,451 --> 00:01:03,310
Slow motion on the braking.

27
00:01:03,310 --> 00:01:07,713
The velocity arrow shrinks, and the
acceleration arrow points backward.

28
00:01:08,113 --> 00:01:10,085
So the shape tells the story.

29
00:01:10,085 --> 00:01:11,560
Up means speeding up.

30
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:12,833
Flat means steady.

31
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:14,451
Down means slowing down.

32
00:01:15,101 --> 00:01:16,240
Side by side.

33
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,799
On a position-time graph, a flat
line means the bicycle is parked.

34
00:01:21,199 --> 00:01:25,887
On a velocity-time graph, a flat line
means it moves at a steady speed.

35
00:01:26,487 --> 00:01:27,565
Now you try.

36
00:01:27,565 --> 00:01:31,282
A velocity-time graph is flat
at six metres per second.

37
00:01:31,282 --> 00:01:33,867
Is the bicycle at rest, or moving?

38
00:01:33,867 --> 00:01:35,207
Pause and decide.

39
00:01:38,407 --> 00:01:39,307
Moving!

40
00:01:39,136 --> 00:01:42,371
Its velocity stays at
six metres per second.

41
00:01:42,371 --> 00:01:44,634
Only its acceleration is zero.

42
00:01:45,284 --> 00:01:48,433
Now, why does the slope
give the acceleration?

43
00:01:48,433 --> 00:01:49,519
Step by step.

44
00:01:49,869 --> 00:01:50,782
Step one.

45
00:01:50,782 --> 00:01:55,579
Acceleration is the change in
velocity, divided by the time taken.

46
00:01:56,329 --> 00:01:57,229
Step two.

47
00:01:57,138 --> 00:02:01,774
On the graph, the change in velocity
is the rise, and the time taken is

48
00:02:01,774 --> 00:02:02,674
the run.

49
00:02:03,283 --> 00:02:04,265
Step three.

50
00:02:04,265 --> 00:02:06,081
Slope is rise over run.

51
00:02:06,081 --> 00:02:08,494
So the slope is the acceleration.

52
00:02:09,244 --> 00:02:14,048
For Aarav, six over four is one
point five metres per second squared.

53
00:02:14,048 --> 00:02:17,499
When braking, minus six
over three is minus two.

54
00:02:18,199 --> 00:02:20,152
Now the area under the line.

55
00:02:20,152 --> 00:02:21,707
Here is the big idea.

56
00:02:22,057 --> 00:02:25,758
At a steady velocity, displacement
is velocity times time.

57
00:02:25,758 --> 00:02:28,571
On the graph, that is height times width.

58
00:02:28,571 --> 00:02:30,280
The area of a rectangle.

59
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:32,491
The units agree too.

60
00:02:32,491 --> 00:02:35,650
Metres per second, times
seconds, gives metres.

61
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:41,079
So for the whole ride, the first triangle
gives twelve metres, the rectangle

62
00:02:41,079 --> 00:02:43,763
twenty four, and the last triangle nine.

63
00:02:44,463 --> 00:02:46,338
Forty five metres in total.

64
00:02:46,338 --> 00:02:48,163
The meter never showed it.

65
00:02:48,163 --> 00:02:49,278
The graph did.

66
00:02:49,928 --> 00:02:51,024
Remember this.

67
00:02:51,024 --> 00:02:54,600
The slope of a velocity-time
graph is the acceleration.

68
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,233
The area under it is the displacement.

69
00:02:57,933 --> 00:02:59,071
One more shape.

70
00:02:59,071 --> 00:03:01,859
What if the line goes below the time axis?

71
00:03:02,209 --> 00:03:04,248
A trolley is pushed up a slope.

72
00:03:04,248 --> 00:03:06,746
It slows down, stops, and rolls back.

73
00:03:07,146 --> 00:03:09,745
Below the axis, the velocity is negative.

74
00:03:09,745 --> 00:03:12,677
The trolley is moving
backward, down the slope.

75
00:03:13,077 --> 00:03:13,977
Your turn.

76
00:03:13,950 --> 00:03:17,665
The line reaches zero at four
seconds, then goes below.

77
00:03:17,665 --> 00:03:19,673
What happens at four seconds?

78
00:03:19,673 --> 00:03:20,908
Pause and think.

79
00:03:24,108 --> 00:03:25,315
It turns around!

80
00:03:25,315 --> 00:03:29,271
For an instant it is at rest,
then it moves the other way.

81
00:03:29,271 --> 00:03:32,540
Area below the axis counts
as negative displacement.

82
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:34,925
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:35,275 --> 00:03:40,320
A bus speeds up evenly from ten to
twenty metres per second in five seconds.

84
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:43,133
Find its acceleration
and its displacement.

85
00:03:43,633 --> 00:03:44,623
Slope first.

86
00:03:44,623 --> 00:03:46,714
Twenty minus ten, over five.

87
00:03:46,714 --> 00:03:49,336
That is two metres per second squared.

88
00:03:50,086 --> 00:03:51,127
Now the area.

89
00:03:51,127 --> 00:03:52,863
The shape is a trapezium.

90
00:03:52,863 --> 00:03:56,285
Half the sum of the parallel
sides, times the width.

91
00:03:57,035 --> 00:03:59,481
Half of ten plus twenty is fifteen.

92
00:03:59,481 --> 00:04:01,866
Fifteen times five is seventy five.

93
00:04:02,616 --> 00:04:06,364
So the bus covers seventy five
metres in those five seconds.

94
00:04:07,014 --> 00:04:08,480
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:08,830 --> 00:04:13,745
Many students say a line sloping down
means the object is moving backward.

96
00:04:14,095 --> 00:04:14,995
Not true.

97
00:04:14,929 --> 00:04:18,161
Above the axis, the
velocity is still positive.

98
00:04:18,161 --> 00:04:20,347
It moves forward, just slower.

99
00:04:20,347 --> 00:04:22,959
It moves backward only below the axis.

100
00:04:23,609 --> 00:04:25,112
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:25,112 --> 00:04:26,504
As graph questions.

102
00:04:26,504 --> 00:04:31,366
Read the slope for acceleration, and
the area for displacement or distance.

103
00:04:31,966 --> 00:04:33,242
Exam level now.

104
00:04:33,242 --> 00:04:37,859
A particle's velocity falls evenly
from plus eight to minus four metres

105
00:04:37,859 --> 00:04:39,859
per second in six seconds.

106
00:04:39,859 --> 00:04:41,968
What distance does it cover?

107
00:04:41,968 --> 00:04:43,298
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:48,698 --> 00:04:50,148
The answer is B.

109
00:04:50,148 --> 00:04:51,351
Twenty metres.

110
00:04:51,851 --> 00:04:56,297
The slope is minus two, so the
line crosses zero at four seconds.

111
00:04:56,297 --> 00:04:59,170
The area above the axis is sixteen metres.

112
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:01,717
The area below is four metres.

113
00:05:01,717 --> 00:05:03,803
Distance adds both, so twenty.

114
00:05:03,803 --> 00:05:07,044
Twelve is the displacement,
and that is the trap.

115
00:05:07,644 --> 00:05:09,107
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:09,107 --> 00:05:10,610
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:13,810 --> 00:05:16,808
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:05:17,208 --> 00:05:18,108
One.

119
00:05:17,717 --> 00:05:21,114
What does a flat line on a
velocity-time graph mean?

120
00:05:22,014 --> 00:05:22,914
Two.

121
00:05:22,507 --> 00:05:25,989
Why does the area under the
line give the displacement?

122
00:05:26,889 --> 00:05:27,789
Three.

123
00:05:27,492 --> 00:05:30,695
What happens when the line
crosses the time axis?

124
00:05:31,795 --> 00:05:36,122
Next time: in a bus you feel the
braking, but never the speed.

125
00:05:36,122 --> 00:05:37,022
Why?

126
00:05:36,632 --> 00:05:39,290
We find out with acceleration-time graphs.
